The advantage of daggers - They do not disappear when thrown
except in the rare case where they are eaten by the target
monster.  Having daggers of wood handles that.

The advantage of arrows for rangers - The Longbow makes them
so it is very rare to run out.

The disadvantage of darts - They only come from traps and a
small number of monster types.  Even blessed and with maxed
out luck they can run out.

But that's only the issue of quantity.  The other issue is the
cost of skill slots to acheive multi-fire.  Getting to Expert costs
5-6 slots for each weapons class depending on whether you get
one in your initial inventory.  Those 11 slots to get your ranger
to Expert in both doesn't leave a lot for darts, or spending slots
on darts makes daggers and/or bow more expensive.  It doable
but many perfer to enhance some types of melee weapons
and/or spell schools.  Try the task of building a skill slot strategy
for each character class and it ends up interesting.

> One thing to be said for choosing arrows over either is that should your
> attack fail to kill a monster, the monster is much less likely to be
> able to use your heavily-enchanted ammunition against you.

Which is an inadequacy of the code.  Or maybe a way to make a
very difficult game possible ...

The main advantage as the game progresses is the player's
equipment trends toward more and more enchanted but the
equipment carried by the monsters the player faces stays centered
near +0.  The power of the monsters trends to match an unarmed
and unarmored player with specific notable exceptions as the game
progresses.



